-- #1110 ARCH A-1 PR "A": the DISCRIMINATING fixture for `Mono.TRigid`.
--
-- `Bad`'s field type names `zzz`, which is not a parameter of the declaration,
-- so `fromAstTypeE`'s `TyVar` fallback mints a rigid for it.  `use` then makes
-- that rigid the RECEIVER of an interface method, which is the only way to force
-- the rigid to be used as a GOAL-SIDE DISPATCH KEY.
--
-- WHAT THIS PINS, and why the wording of the error is the whole assertion:
-- `headTyconMono` answers `Some "zzz"` for a rigid, so `allConcreteHeads` is
-- True, the goal is treated as concrete, and `checkOneCallObligation` reports
-- the no-instance error naming the head.  If a `TRigid` were swallowed by that
-- function's `_ => None` wildcard, `allConcreteHeads` would go False and the
-- call would route to `checkUndeterminedObligations` instead — a DIFFERENT
-- diagnostic.  So the golden discriminates the dangerous case; an exit code or
-- a mere "it errors" assertion would not.
--
-- Deliberately an ORDINARY rigid name.  `__tupleN__` is also a legal rigid
-- spelling and collides with the builtin tuple head — that forgery is tracked
-- separately (DICT-SEMANTICS §8 I6.2(b), OWED-#1110) and is NOT what this
-- fixture is for.
--
-- ⚠️ This fixture asserts an ANSWER, not a correct answer.  Handing a rigid
-- variable out as a dispatch key violates §8 I6.1; PR "A" is the carrier step
-- and preserves it deliberately.  When the follow-on lands, this golden MOVES —
-- that is the intended self-drain, not a regression.

interface Q a where
  qq : a -> Int

data Bad = Bad zzz

use : Bad -> Int
use (Bad v) = qq v
